// joint — THE FORWARD GATE FOR `SPEC` **I6**, AND IT IS RED ON PURPOSE.
//
// `I6`: *a part's pose is a **pure function** of its joint values; wheel angle `= travel/radius`.*
// Control named by the spec: *spin a wheel off-travel → it skids.*
//
// Nothing here asserts anything yet, because **`G1` is unbuilt** — there is no body, no part, no
// joint. This gate exists BEFORE its subject, the way `rt_trip` was written red at `S7` and held
// red by the runner until `rebuild` landed (`DESIGN.md` §8.0). `tools/run_tests.sh` runs it every
// time through `run_red`, which **asserts the redness**: it must not print `JOINT OK`, and it must
// print `JOINT RED` for the stated reason. If it ever goes green by accident the runner FAILS.
//
// WHY WRITE IT NOW. `ASSESSMENT.md` §5 item 0: after `G2` and the foxel, **nothing is armed**.
// `SURFACE_LANDED` sat false from `S4` to `G2` and fired on exactly the step it was aimed at
// (`X62`) — better than a permanently red suite and better than a forgotten TODO. `M0` is closed
// and the body is next, so the tripwire for the body belongs in the tree *before* the body does.
//
// AND THE HARDER REASON. **`M0`'s method does not transfer to motion unmodified.** Every gate in
// this repo compares committed bytes to recomputed bytes; a moving part has no corpus. What DOES
// transfer is the shape of the claim: `I6` says the pose is a *function* of the joints, which is
// the body's `law D` — and a function that is also injective gives `recover(pose(j)) = j`, the
// body's round trip. Whether it IS injective is an OPEN QUESTION and this gate must not presume
// it; see §3.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT THIS GATE MUST ASSERT WHEN `G1` LANDS. Written now, while the shape is
// being decided, so it constrains the design instead of being fitted to it.
//
//   1  PURITY — pose is a function of the joint values ALONE.
//      Reach the same joint vector by two DIFFERENT PATHS — set it directly, and set it after
//      visiting other values — and require the two poses byte-identical. Also: calling `pose`
//      twice changes nothing, and no elapsed time, frame count or call order appears in the
//      result.
//      CONTROL: an accumulating implementation (`pose += delta`) must FAIL the two-path check.
//      Without a second path the check is vacuous — one call always equals itself.
//
//   2  WHEEL ANGLE = TRAVEL / RADIUS, and the skid is the instrument.
//      For a wheel of radius `r` rolled a distance `d`, the angle is `d/r` and the contact point
//      does not slip: `skid = |r·θ − d| = 0`, exactly, with no tolerance (law **P4** — this is a
//      derived quantity, not a measured one).
//      CONTROL: drive `θ` and `d` INDEPENDENTLY — the spec's own "spin a wheel off-travel" — and
//      require `skid > 0`. A zero skid on a wheel that was never turned is not a result.
//      ⚠ `r` is a length: state it in metres and register it (`SPEC` **L8**, `tests/scale.loft`).
//
//   3  THE JOINT ROUND TRIP — **DISSOLVED, not answered.** ⚠ It was listed here as an open
//      question (is `joints → pose` injective?). The user's decision of 2026-07-24 removed the
//      question rather than settling it: *"treat bodies we compose like meshes, so we define the
//      bones and the limitations in the joints between the bones, never the actual pose."*
//
//      An authored body is therefore a **RIG** — `⟨bones, joint limits⟩` — and a pose is never
//      authored. So the body's round trip is on the **rig text**, `write(read(R)) = R`, the same
//      shape as a stencil's; nothing ever needs to recover joints FROM a pose. That recovery is
//      inverse kinematics, which is non-unique by nature, and the design simply never asks for it.
//
//      **This is the project's own pattern, a fifth time** (`X51` door, `X58` level, `X59`
//      terrain, `X60` run): *the round trip survives a new feature exactly when that feature
//      lands in a slot the recovery does not read.* Here the feature is MOTION, and it lands
//      outside the authored model entirely.
//
//      What survives from this section is the load-bearing half, and it is §1: forward kinematics
//      must be a **pure function**, or `I9`'s byte-identical replay is unreachable.
//
//   ⚠  FLEX IS JOINTS, NOT DEFORMATION — the design constraint most likely to be missed when `G1`
//      is written, because the reflex is to reach for skinning. User, 2026-07-24, on the aeroplane
//      case: *"in any realistic scenario the wings have to be different defined objects — they are
//      attached but not rigid, otherwise they would fail immediately. They can bend quite a bit
//      upwards."* A part that bends is **more bones**, not a soft bone. So hexbody needs **no
//      vertex deformation, no skinning weights, no soft body**, and §1 survives a flexing wing
//      unchanged: a bent wing is still a pure function of its joint values. See `SPEC` **I-POSE**.
//
//   ⚠  AND `L15` BOUNDS WHAT MAY BE BUILT HERE AT ALL. A train, a robot, an aeroplane and a
//      terminal dock have different underlying logic, and no honest abstraction covers all of
//      them — so hexbody supplies bones, limits and the pose NOW, and never the domain's
//      behaviour. Domain simulations are legitimate work, as **consumers in their own projects**.
//      `tests/scope.loft` enforces the naming half of that boundary on `src/`.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// HOW TO TURN IT GREEN. Land `G1`, write §1 and §2 as real checks against the real API, flip
// `BODY_LANDED`, and MOVE this row out of `run_red` into the runner's table. The runner enforces
// the last step: while the row is in `run_red`, printing `JOINT OK` is itself a failure.

// ⚠ G1 HAS LANDED — this gate was RED (`run_red`) until `src/hexjoint.loft` existed; now it runs
// the real §1/§2 checks and lives in the runner's table.  The tripwire it carried (scan `src/` for
// a body verb) did its job: it fired the moment the body was declared, forcing these checks to be
// written rather than left as a pending stub, exactly as designed above.  Its scaffolding
// (`BODY_LANDED`, `body_verbs`, the src scan) is deleted — a landed subject needs a real gate, not
// a tripwire.
use hexjoint;

const TAU = 6.283185307179586;
fn fabs(x: float) -> float { if x < 0.0 { 0.0 - x } else { x } }

fn main() {
  ok = true;
  println("=== joint — SPEC I6: a pose is a PURE FUNCTION of joint values (G1) ===");

  // ── 1. PURITY — pose depends on the CURRENT joint value alone, not on the path to it ──
  //
  // Reach the same joint value two ways: directly, and after visiting other values.  `pose_of` is
  // stateless, so both give a BYTE-IDENTICAL pose (`==`, no tolerance — `I6` is exact, `P4`).  And
  // calling it twice changes nothing.
  println("");
  println("1. PURITY — same joint value by two DIFFERENT paths -> byte-identical pose");
  vgoal = 0.37;
  lx = 1.4;
  ly = -0.6;
  (ax, ay) = pose_of(vgoal, lx, ly);                 // path A: set the value directly
  // path B: "visit" 0.1, then 0.62, then land on vgoal.  A pure pose reads only the last value.
  pose_of(0.1, lx, ly);
  pose_of(0.62, lx, ly);
  (bx, by) = pose_of(vgoal, lx, ly);
  path_eq = ax == bx && ay == by;
  (cx, cy) = pose_of(vgoal, lx, ly);                 // and again — idempotent
  idem = cx == ax && cy == ay;
  println("   path A -> ({ax}, {ay})");
  println("   path B -> ({bx}, {by})   byte-identical: {path_eq}");
  println("   called twice, unchanged: {idem}");
  if !path_eq || !idem {
    println("  FAIL: pose depends on the path or the call count — it is not a pure function of the"
            + " joint value, and I9's byte-identical replay is then unreachable"); ok = false;
  }

  // CONTROL — an ACCUMULATING pose (the tempting stateful implementation) must FAIL the two-path
  // check, or "path-independent" is vacuous: one call always equals itself.
  accA = wheel_angle(vgoal);                          // path A: one step to vgoal
  accB = wheel_angle(0.1) + wheel_angle(0.62) + wheel_angle(vgoal);  // path B: summed visits
  println("   CONTROL an accumulating pose differs by path: accA {accA} vs accB {accB}"
          + " -> {accA != accB}");
  if accA == accB {
    println("  FAIL: the control did not fire — even an accumulator agreed across the two paths,"
            + " so the purity check above proves nothing"); ok = false;
  }

  // ── 2. ROLLING — wheel angle = travel/radius, and the slip is MACHINE-ε, not a tuned bound ──
  //
  // The joint value is DERIVED from travel (`value = travel/(2πr)`), so the no-slip identity holds
  // by construction.  ⚠ But it holds in ℝ, not bit-for-bit in f64: `travel → value → r·θ` is a
  // float round-trip, so `skid` is ~1 ulp of `travel`, not 0.  This is the BODY'S SOLE FLOAT STEP —
  // the wheel's `ε_seam` (`I-FSEAM`, `X53`) — not a `P4` violation (P4 governs the integer R1
  // geometry trip; kinematics is the float frame).  The bound below is machine ε, and the control
  // shows it is a real bound and not vacuous: an off-travel spin skids by whole units.
  println("");
  println("2. ROLLING — wheel angle = travel/radius, skid is machine-eps (the body's sole float step)");
  radius = 2.5;
  worst = 0.0;
  npt = 0;
  for i in 0..41 {
    travel = (i as float) * 0.37;                    // 41 points along the roll
    v = wheel_value(travel, radius, 0.0);
    sk = wheel_skid(radius, v, travel);
    if sk > worst { worst = sk; }
    npt = npt + 1;
  }
  // ~1 ulp of the largest travel (14.8) is ~1.8e-15; a bound of 1e-12 is machine ε with headroom,
  // and 12+ orders of magnitude below the control's whole-unit skid — so it is a bound, not a knob.
  const EPS_ROLL = 0.000000000001;
  println("   {npt} points along the roll: worst skid = {worst}  (bound {EPS_ROLL}, machine eps)");
  if worst > EPS_ROLL {
    println("  FAIL: a derived wheel value slipped beyond machine eps — the roll is not deriving"
            + " the angle from the travel"); ok = false;
  }

  // CONTROL — drive theta INDEPENDENTLY of travel (SPEC I6's own "spin a wheel off-travel"): a
  // value that does not come from this travel must skid.
  offv = wheel_value(10.0, radius, 0.0);             // the value for travel 10 ...
  offskid = wheel_skid(radius, offv, 3.0);           // ... but applied at travel 3
  println("   CONTROL spin off-travel (value for d=10, applied at d=3): skid = {offskid}"
          + " -> {offskid > 0.0}");
  if offskid <= 0.0 {
    println("  FAIL: the control did not fire — an off-travel spin did not skid, so skid=0 above"
            + " is not measuring the rolling constraint"); ok = false;
  }

  // ── 3. THE DOORSTEP — a joint value fits within its limits, and a refusal OFFERS ──
  //
  // `K-FIT` for a joint.  Unlike a nominal material id (`X68`), a joint value is ORDINAL, so a
  // refusal owes an OFFER of the nearest admissible value, with its residual.
  println("");
  println("3. THE DOORSTEP — a joint value fits within its limits; a refusal offers the nearest");
  hinge = joint_new(0.0, -0.25, 0.25);               // a hinge limited to +-1/4 turn
  inlim = joint_fits(hinge, 0.1);
  over = joint_fits(hinge, 0.9);
  offer = joint_offer(hinge, 0.9);
  resid = joint_residual(hinge, 0.9);
  println("   0.1 within +-0.25: {inlim}   0.9 within: {over}   offer {offer} residual {resid}");
  if !inlim || over {
    println("  FAIL: the limit does not distinguish in-range from out-of-range"); ok = false;
  }
  if !joint_fits(hinge, offer) || offer != 0.25 {
    println("  FAIL: the offer is not itself admissible, or not the nearest limit"); ok = false;
  }

  // ── 4. THE JOINT ROUND TRIP IS DISSOLVED, not asserted ────────────────────
  println("");
  println("4. the joint ROUND TRIP is DISSOLVED (I-POSE), and this gate asserts nothing about it:");
  println("   an authored body is a RIG (bones + joint limits) and never a pose, so nothing");
  println("   recovers joints FROM a pose -- that is inverse kinematics, and the design never");
  println("   asks for it. The trip is on the rig text, phase A of plans/m1-moving-body.");

  println("");
  if ok { println("=== JOINT OK ==="); }
  else { println("=== JOINT BROKEN ==="); }
}
